\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Remark}
\title{Scalar factorization, localization and gluing}
\date{2026-10-31}

\p{The [retained-variance example](fdip-004P) tests scalar
factorization: for specified functions #{c:X\to Y} and
#{r:X\to\mathbb R}, it asks whether #{r=F\circ c} for a
function on #{c(X)}. The [fiber criterion](fdip-0042) answers
exactly this question, without supplying continuity or any
categorical structure.}

\p{Functorial factorization instead starts with [categories](tt-0002) and
[functors](tt-0014) #{U:\mathcal B\to\mathcal D} and
#{O:\mathcal B\to\mathcal A}, and asks for
#{\overline O:\mathcal D\to\mathcal A} and a
[natural isomorphism](tt-001H)
#{O\cong\overline O\circ U}. A \em{localization} at a declared
class #{W} of arrows has an additional universal property: it
inverts those arrows and is universal among functors that do so
\citet{Section 4.27, Tag 04VB}{stacks2017stacks}.
A generic [forgetful functor](tt-003R) is not thereby a localization.
Mathlib's \leanref{CategoryTheory.Localization.lift} requires
both a localization functor and a functor that inverts #{W};
these hypotheses are absent from the scalar example.}

\p{Local-to-global descent requires a site with covers,
restriction maps and compatible data on overlaps, together with
an effective gluing statement. For category-valued data, a stack
also requires a fibred category and sheaves of morphisms
\citet{Definition 8.4.1, Tag 0268}{stacks2017stacks}.
The [enriched background groupoid](fdip-000X) and the scalar
variance calculation supply none of this cover-and-gluing structure.
The groupoid is a category of finite backgrounds, not necessarily a
finite category. No local-to-global descent theorem follows.}
